    a: 'You can start with a BA or BSc in Psychology, but to practise as a clinical psychologist you must be on the Central Rehabilitation Register under the Rehabilitation Council of India, which needs a recognised qualification beyond the bachelor\'s. "Counsellor" is not a registered title yet. Ask each college which route it actually leads to.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I switch to science or commerce later if I take humanities?',
    a: 'Switching streams after Class 12 is difficult for science-only routes such as MBBS or B.Tech. But many colleges take humanities students into commerce-side programmes such as BBA or BCom, and humanities students can move into management, design, law and psychology, and into data skills through short courses or a later master\'s.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I drop a year to crack CLAT or a top exam?',
    a: 'Only if the year has a plan: a clear target score, a checkpoint date, a fallback college and one skill-building output. Check the age gates first: NDA, IPM and UCEED can close after a drop year, and the Bar Council\'s age cap for 5-year LLB is under Supreme Court review, so confirm before planning a late attempt. Use an honest mock score, not hope, to decide.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which exams can a humanities student take with the least maths?',
    a: 'CUET UG for a BA (Hons) other than Economics, CLAT (quantitative techniques is about 10% of the paper at school level), NID DAT and UCEED for design, and NCET for teaching are the lightest on maths. NIFT\'s General Ability Test and the hotel management test each carry a small quantitative section, so plan for basic school maths rather than none. NDA and IPMAT are the two that need real preparation.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a drop year close any doors for humanities students?',
    a: 'It can. NDA, IPM at the IIMs and UCEED all have age or attempt limits, so a second attempt after a drop year may be the last one. NCET, NCHM JEE, CUET UG and CLAT have had no upper age limit, though the Bar Council\'s age cap for the 5-year LLB is under Supreme Court review. Check each notice before you decide to repeat.',
