  <h2 id="answer">The direct answer: choose by deadline, maths comfort and proof</h2>

  <p>
    Most guides on career options after 12th humanities give you a list of twenty jobs. That is not the
    hard part. The hard part is that your Class 12 result arrives, relatives start talking, and two or three
    of the best doors have already closed or are about to.
  </p>

  <p>
    A better order is the calendar first, then the maths gate, then the paths ranked by how far they can
    grow, then real pay with the number behind it.
  </p>

  <div class="callout callout--amber">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Questions students actually ask</p>
    <ul class="callout__list">
      <li>"What are the best fields after humanities which have scope?"</li>
      <li>"If I choose arts, will I still get a good graduation and job later?"</li>
      <li>"Is there any exam I can give if I have no maths?"</li>
      <li>"My parents say arts means UPSC or nothing. Is that true?"</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout__footer">
      Short answers: yes, scope exists; yes, graduation and jobs follow; several exams need no maths; and UPSC
      is one route of many, not the only respectable one.
    </p>
  </div>

