        <li>The boring 80% is revision, client correction and packaging, not only the creative part.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>The expensive wrong turns</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Paying for a private BA LLB or hotel management course without checking the college's placement denominator.</li>
    <li>A drop year for one exam with no fallback and no skill built in that year.</li>
    <li>Choosing IPM or NDA without a maths plan.</li>
    <li>Taking an education loan for a course that pays modestly at entry at many colleges.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>A first test that fits your pace</h3>

  <p>
    Pick one path and do a small real thing. Sit a full timed CLAT or CUET mock, write a one-page brief on
    a real judgment or news story, submit three sketchbook pieces, or spend a day with a working teacher. Do
    it for as long as it takes to get honest feedback. Some students need a few weeks, others a couple of
    months, and both are normal.
  </p>

  <div class="gates-block">
    <p class="gates-block__title">The 3 Gates: pass these before you commit money or a drop year.</p>
    <div class="gates-row">
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 1</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of skill</span>
        <p>One finished mock paper, brief or portfolio piece from the path you are considering.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 2</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of communication</span>
        <p>A one-to-two-minute explanation of why this path suits you, in words your family can follow.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="gate">
        <span class="gate__num">Gate 3</span>
        <span class="gate__name">Proof of value</span>
        <p>Feedback from three people working in that field on what is still missing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">The money filter: the college is not the whole budget</h2>

  <p>
    A degree is one line in your education budget, not the whole of it. As a strict planning heuristic, keep
    the college fee near 10% of the total education budget. If your family's complete budget is Rs 10 lakh,
    that is roughly Rs 1 lakh for the college and Rs 9 lakh for living security, tools, books, projects,
    certifications and later upskilling. Treat spending above roughly 20% as a serious caution that needs
    course-specific proof, such as a verified placement record or a legal requirement. Both numbers are
    planning heuristics, not universal facts.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When paying more can make sense</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A top National Law University where the course-specific placement record is strong and verified.</li>
        <li>Licensed routes where a recognised degree is legally needed, such as law or clinical psychology.</li>
        <li>Scarce facilities such as a design studio or media lab that you cannot rebuild yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">When it is likely waste</div>
      <ul>
        <li>A private college with no published median and only a brochure headline.</li>
        <li>A fee that leaves no money for tools, internships and a portfolio.</li>
        <li>A loan against a path with modest entry pay and no backup skill.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Many humanities skills can be built cheaply. Writing, research, basic design and digital marketing can
    be practised with good YouTube channels, official documentation and reputable free or low-cost online
    courses, and then shown through real work. A certificate is not proof; a finished piece of work is.
    Law, clinical psychology and other licensed routes still need the formal degree.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ai">AI and humanities careers: what shrinks and what holds</h2>

  <p>
    Every humanities path now has an AI question. The honest answer is that routine output shrinks first and
    responsibility holds.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Path</th>
          <th>What AI compresses</th>
          <th>What still needs you</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {aiRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Path"><strong>{item.path}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What AI compresses">{item.pressure}</td>
            <td data-label="What still needs you">{item.holds}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    Start now by using AI as a study partner: summarise a reading, then check every claim against the
    source, and never submit a citation you have not verified. Later, as tools mature, the leverage grows
    for people who pair a deep subject with a clear workflow. AI helps a strong student more; it does not
    replace judgment.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      Add communication to every path. English is a multiplier, whatever your school medium: understand the
      work, do the work, explain the work, then improve the language around it. A Telugu-, Hindi- or
      Tamil-medium student can think in one language and present in another.
    </p>
  </div>

