  <h3>If you are a parent</h3>

  <p>
    Your job is to improve the quality of the decision, not to replace it. Ask better questions: what will
    you finish this month, what is the college's median and denominator, what happens if the exam goes
    badly? A placement report is not a promise, and a relative's stories are not data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>

  <p>
    A good first step is simple. Pick two paths, take one mock or brief for each, list the deadlines and age
    limits for those doors, and write down what your fallback is if an exam goes badly. If you want a structured
    plan, choose
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">continuous
    career guidance</a>. It builds a high-value skill portfolio around your chosen path, so your first
    income arrives earlier and compounds toward earlier financial freedom. If you would rather start smaller,
    try the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Keep reading based on your situation: the full
    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/courses-for-arts-and-humanities-students-after-12th/">course-by-course
    list for arts students</a>, the honest
    <a href="/blog/career-options/best-paying-jobs-for-humanities-students-india/">pay ranking for
    humanities graduates</a>, the
    <a href="/blog/career-options/upsc-alternative-careers-for-humanities-students-india/">UPSC alternatives
    for humanities students</a>, or
    <a href="/blog/college-degrees/should-i-take-drop-year-after-12th/">whether a drop year makes sense</a>.
    If you feel stuck, start with
    <a href="/blog/career-guidance/what-to-do-after-12th-confused/">what to do after 12th when you are
    confused</a>.
  </p>

